    <h2>When the DQ Sandwich Makes the Most Sense</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For kids:</strong> Easy to hold, no cone tip to drip, familiar wafer-and-cream flavor. At $1.49 it is one of the most affordable treats to add to a <a href="/categories/kids-meals">Kids Meal</a> order.</li>
      <li><strong>As a budget add-on:</strong> When you want something sweet after a main meal but do not want to spend $3.49–$4.49 on a Blizzard, the DQ Sandwich delivers at $1.49.</li>
      <li><strong>For calorie-conscious guests:</strong> At 190 calories it is the lightest frozen treat on the menu — 40 fewer than a regular Vanilla Cone and about 230 fewer than a Mini Blizzard.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Anatomy of the Signature Chocolate Wafer</h2>
    <p>Unlike crunchy supermarket cookies that crack under pressure and squeeze ice cream out the sides, Dairy Queen's specialized wafers are engineered with microscopic perforations. This allows moisture from the vanilla soft serve to subtly temper the wafer biscuit, creating a cohesive, chewy mouthfeel that bites cleanly from the first nibble to the last.</p>

